the french president looked relaxed as  he voted in the coastal resort of le  tuke but emmanuel macron faces a  challenging week after the first round  of france's parliamentary elections his  centrist alliance is on track to win  most seats but not necessarily the  majority he needs to push ahead with his  reform agenda  we are the only political movement in a  position to win a majority in the  national assembly we have one week  before us to rally our forces one week  to persuade one week to win a strong and  clear majority only such a strong and  clear majority will enable us to respond  to the urgent issues which affect the  daily lives of french people  a new left-wing block that includes  greens communists and socialists created  by far-left leader jealous  has emerged as the main rival to  macron's party it's attracted voters  concerned over the cost of living and  climate change  i call on our people viewing this result  and this opportunity for our personal  lives and for the nation i call on  people to go out and reject once and for  all the funeral project of macron  the far right and conservative parties  trailed the centrists and the left in an  election that's largely failed to  capture voters imagination turnout was  at an historic low with less than half  of those eligible casting their ballot  this vote comes less than two months  after the presidential campaign so  there's definitely a sense of election  fatigue it's also the case though that a  growing number of people in france feel  fed up of politics and politicians and  increasingly disengaged from the  political process  with a tight race ahead the parties will  step up their campaigns in a critical  test for the president the big lesson of  this first round for emmanuel macron is  that he's not certain to have an  absolute majority in parliament and that  will make his life more difficult if he  hasn't got one so he he's tried to  hammer this message in the last few days  before the first round and he will be  trying to push that message for the  second round give me a majority  in one week voters will return to the  polls for the second round of the  election and will determine how france  is governed for the next five years  whether there will be a swing to the  left or more of the same natasha butler  al jazeera paris
 

